{# Suggested heading for the host SKILL.md:
   ### Step 1: Ingest

   Place under the `## Workflow` section, immediately before
   `### Step 2: Drive the loop`.

   Variables (must be passed by the includer):
     prefix          — uppercase shell-variable prefix, e.g. "SB"
     shim            — uppercase wrapper-script variable, e.g. "LOOM";
                       must resolve to the loomv2 CLI (loom.sh) or a
                       wrapper forwarding args verbatim
     skill_name      — kebab-case skill name shown in tiling labels
     target          — placeholder for the primary parameter, e.g. "<op>"
     loom_root_var   — uppercase shell-variable holding the absolute
                       path to the skill's `loom/` folder
     set_assignments — pre-rendered string of `--set K=V` fragments the
                       host skill wants seeded into the entry task's
                       `input.yaml`
#}
1. Set tiling activity:
   ```bash
   ${{ prefix }}_TILING activity set "{{ skill_name }}({{ target }}): Ingest"
   ```
2. Run the DEFAULT single-call ingest — wipe any existing workdir,
   init fresh, and seed the entry task's `input.yaml` in one shot:
   ```bash
   ${{ prefix }}_{{ shim }} runtime init "${{ prefix }}_WD" \
       --loom-root "${{ prefix }}_{{ loom_root_var }}" \
       --force {{ set_assignments }}
   ```
   No per-skill Python or wrapper logic is required — the loomv2 CLI
   owns wipe, init, and entry-task seeding. If the entry task cannot
   be seeded via `--set` (for example, the graph's entry task has an
   `input:` mapping in `graph.yaml`), the host skill drops
   `--force --set` and writes `input.yaml` by hand before the first
   `runtime next` — supported as the escape hatch, not the default.
3. Set tiling activity to Ingest done:
   ```bash
   ${{ prefix }}_TILING activity set "{{ skill_name }}({{ target }}): Ingest done"
   ```
